WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Film Courage: How many film submissions&nbsp;
did you watch total from your running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.049
NFFTY [National Film Festival for Talented Youth]?

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:13.860
Jesse Harris, Filmmaker: I think I probably&nbsp;
watched nearly 10,000 films, a lot. We were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.820
getting thousands of submissions. In the first&nbsp;
few years I was watching every single one of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:27.780
Eventually we got more people involved to do&nbsp;
pre-screening and start I don't want to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.280
eliminating but limiting films but we were&nbsp;
always very fair. We had multiple people watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.480
every film so it wasn't just one person deciding&nbsp;
on it. But yeah I watched a ton of films while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.220
doing the festival. I think it definitely had&nbsp;
an impact on me because for one I saw a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:47.400
really bad films of course and so I saw what&nbsp;
not to do and how to learn from that. Then I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.620
saw every once in a while so I saw some incredible&nbsp;
movies that were just like mind blown! I was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.620
how did someone my age make this and so it was&nbsp;
it was probably again this like amazing almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.260
film school like experience but in an even more&nbsp;
probably modern way of just watching all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:10.980
films from all over the world of all different&nbsp;
kinds raw and super professional and getting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.220
one hand analyzing them as a viewer and being&nbsp;
like would people like this and should this be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.200
in the festival but also as a filmmaker being like&nbsp;
wow look what they did or whew, look what they did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.840
and how we should not do that and so it probably&nbsp;
I'm sure contributed a lot in terms of expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.420
my filmmaking knowledge college and abilities&nbsp;
after watching so many films for so many years.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.020
Film Courage: Then what do you think&nbsp;
makes a bad movie? What were some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.980
the things where you could almost tell&nbsp;
within the first maybe 30 seconds?

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:45.300
Jesse: I think it was just not telling&nbsp;
an authentic story, someone trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.600
copy something that they had seen already like&nbsp;
making action spoof on some movie they liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.500
or doing something probably the mistakes we all&nbsp;
do which is like trying to copy something and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:08.340
having any original I deal with it and I think&nbsp;
to just sometimes trying to do maybe too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.340
because we had plenty of films that were very&nbsp;
simple made with no money and sometimes those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.800
were some of the best films but then&nbsp;
you had someone who was maybe trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.760
to do two something too ambitious for&nbsp;
what they the resources they had maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.280
there was always issues with acting of course&nbsp;
performances because you're probably limited on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.460
who you can get there was technical challenges&nbsp;
but I honestly don't think a test the technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.140
ability of a film is always really the&nbsp;
best judge of it you could have a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.860
raw film that doesn't have the most amazing&nbsp;
cinematography or whatever but if it has a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.200
great story it can be super successful so I think&nbsp;
it was always just trying to find those stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:57.300
that that were captivating and whether that was&nbsp;
a documentary or a short film or whatever but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:03.900
yeah it was just trying to find those just really&nbsp;
captivating stories from your time with NFFTY what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:09.840
did you see with the submissions that you found&nbsp;
really made a film satisfying any genre I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:18.300
what makes the film satisfying is when it makes&nbsp;
you feel something and whatever that is whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:25.800
it's a sad feeling a happy feeling being scared&nbsp;
whatever I think that that was when I knew we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.840
a good film was what I was after watching hundreds&nbsp;
if not thousands of films you get a little numb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.480
sometimes but then you turn one on while screening&nbsp;
them for the festival and it would just Captivate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.920
you and grab your attention and make you feel&nbsp;
something and if it can make you feel something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.360
after watching tons of other films and being&nbsp;
bored and over over that then it's probably gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.040
Captivate everyone else and so that's what I was&nbsp;
always looking for was what are those just really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.980
compelling films that would make me feel something&nbsp;
whatever that was and I think that's the same with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:01.440
anything now and that's what I try to think&nbsp;
about when I make a film too is just how can I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:09.240
make people feel something and I think for for me&nbsp;
that was always trying to tap into emotion because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.800
that's just what I always went to for some people&nbsp;
it might be comedy or something else but I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.500
if you can if you can do that you can you can make&nbsp;
a great film what about a non-nifty submission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:27.060
what was one that made you feel something however&nbsp;
old you were as a kid my parents actually would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:32.400
either take me to a lot of movies or we had a&nbsp;
video store near our house and so we would rent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.540
lot of films and it was like an independent video&nbsp;
store so it was we'd sometimes find movies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.160
would wouldn't be a blockbuster or something like&nbsp;
that and so I actually probably had a pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.660
Movie experience as a kid watching probably&nbsp;
a lot of movies that were more older than I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.760
really was like not necessarily kid movies but&nbsp;
like I remember being pretty young and watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.280
To Kill a Mockingbird and I remember at first&nbsp;
my mom had rented it and she said you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.600
watch this and we did but it was like it was in&nbsp;
black and white and for the first few minutes I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.520
was like this is going to be boring like what is&nbsp;
this like I was very young at the at the time but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.200
then within probably the first five minutes I was&nbsp;
totally hooked because it is such a captivating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.300
story right has great characters and also there&nbsp;
was these young kids in the movie which I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.400
probably the same ages as them on the screen and&nbsp;
so I think that hooked me and so I think it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.580
it's the same idea where if a movie can grab you&nbsp;
and grab at your emotions or or Captivate you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.440
some way it's gonna have you and then obviously&nbsp;
from there you still have to have the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.900
film be compelling good acting good storytelling&nbsp;
otherwise but I think that's just so important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.740
is just to have something what's something&nbsp;
that's going to grab people and pull you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:47.940
any success stories with NFFTY that really stand&nbsp;
out like whether it was a film that just drew you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:55.200
in or maybe a filmmaker that you were so compelled&nbsp;
by their story their real story definitely I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:03.000
I saw so many amazing films over the many years I&nbsp;
was running the festival one in particular was it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:14.880
was this young female filmmaker from Afghanistan&nbsp;
and she had pulled off making this film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:25.260
and it was a documentary about her life and&nbsp;
this was in I don't know late 2010s I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:33.900
I don't know obviously this was like wars were&nbsp;
going on all over in that area and it was just so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:39.660
profound I guess to see this story from someone&nbsp;
your own age from around the world and then she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:45.420
actually came out to the festival and we got to&nbsp;
meet her which was incredible and there was there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.640
were so many other interesting stories like that&nbsp;
there was I think especially the documentaries at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:58.860
our Festival were so compelling just because they&nbsp;
were so personal and real and raw and so we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.240
amazing short narrative films also all the time&nbsp;
but the documentaries were just so special because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:08.280
they were coming from basically someone your own&nbsp;
age telling some usually some really profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:13.800
amazing story from their life and so those were&nbsp;
always just so compelling and captivating and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:20.160
were able to find films from really all over the&nbsp;
world and all walks of life and it was it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.860
always exciting really every time we would get&nbsp;
a submission in and see what this story was and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.260
a lot of our filmmakers have gone on to to do big&nbsp;
things they've a couple of them made feature films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.980
one we had a short documentary in the festival&nbsp;
several years back that won the Oscar for best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:47.880
short documentary that year so it's it's been this&nbsp;
great I guess you could just call it like a farm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:52.260
team or whatever I guess for for finding these&nbsp;
filmmakers and helping them develop themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.020
but of course not every filmmaker goes on and&nbsp;
has a big success story most of the filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:04.320
if you touched with them now like they probably&nbsp;
won't even be still directing like the reality is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.580
it's just obviously very hard and not everyone&nbsp;
that makes a film when they're in their early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:15.120
20s ends up becoming a director but a lot of them&nbsp;
are working in the industry somewhere or they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.460
just doing something else in their life that is&nbsp;
creative and they're using their skills probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:25.980
as a filmmaker to to tell still tell stories in&nbsp;
some way whether it's marketing or whatever and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:33.120
I think that's the big thing is that no matter&nbsp;
what happens I think a lot of anyone who can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.580
put together a film and go down that path it's&nbsp;
just such a great experience for for learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.800
about yourself but also about how to like tell a&nbsp;
story and Captivate people and I just think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:49.620
a great skill in life that you can use one way&nbsp;
or the other did you see where filmmakers who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:55.200
were overseas maybe did you realize I guess what&nbsp;
privilege we have here in terms of being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.840
make something withstanding certain laws whereas&nbsp;
in other countries probably much much different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:10.080
much more fear yeah for sure there was definitely&nbsp;
films at times we had a few of our filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.680
especially from the Middle East who would get&nbsp;
into our Festival but then they wouldn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.300
be able to travel necessarily to the US to attend&nbsp;
the festival sometimes that was because of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:27.480
own country other times it was because the U.S&nbsp;
wouldn't let them in and it was definitely a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:33.720
time period too again it was like the internet&nbsp;
was was starting to take off more this was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.300
Facebook and social media was was existing but&nbsp;
it still was like the world wasn't as close and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:46.320
connected as it is now and it was always so&nbsp;
captivating captivating when we would get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.500
meet these young filmmakers from around the world&nbsp;
and cure firsthand for them just their experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.460
of their everyday life because it was and that&nbsp;
was what was so special about the festival is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:02.340
you would literally have everyone from a very&nbsp;
privileged probably wealthy family to someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:07.080
who's poor and we had to basically raise money to&nbsp;
bring them out to the festival and we did that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:13.200
some cases and so so but we all were the same in&nbsp;
the end of the day because we're all filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.880
just trying to tell stories and it was such a&nbsp;
special thing to see see everyone from all walks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:26.760
of life interacting and and getting to share and&nbsp;
learn from each other and I think yeah more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.860
anything I think most young people will understand&nbsp;
this because it's in general they're more exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.000
to people probably from around the world but I&nbsp;
think you start to it just makes you realize how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:42.360
similar everyone is and how most of the conflicts&nbsp;
and problems would probably be solved if we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.380
all just like hanging out with each other&nbsp;
a lot more but making movies yeah exactly.

